# `prompt-cycle`, through a widget whose result is a hold's update: `@Echo` steps a hold
# on its argument and gives the hold's update event, which fires in the tick of the step
# (Semantics ch. 7, R3), so `!back` fires in the tick `!step` does, and `!step` merges
# `!back`: one press loops within a tick.
# Promptness is part of a function's type, and the check is local (Jimmy, 13 November,
# ruling PT): `@Echo`'s signature says `prompt`, and the loop is found in `@host` from
# that type alone, never by reading `@Echo`'s body. Without the word, `@Echo` is refused at
# its own body (neg/prompt-unmarked-widget-function).
# Expected text derived by hand; never run.
# Written by Claude on the claude-self-hosting branch; subject to change.
# A plain function is always prompt (ruling PT); only a widget function says it in its type.
emit Ui.*

@Echo: prompt fn !Nat -> @!Nat
fn @Echo !step:
    $seen <- 0 then $seen + 1 on !step
    @ (!Updated $seen)

@host: @unit
@host:
    !press <- Ui.Button($label=$"press")
    !back <- @Echo !step
    let !step = (1 on !press) <| !back
    $last <- 0 then !step
    Ui.Label($text=$"{$last}")
